@* Table's Overview, written by hand after Button's: the URL-first behavior (sorting, paging,
   filters) previewed in each mode, selection in a form, then state in the component (Interactive only),
   data from a database or an API, a failed load, row links and the look as code. From docs/components.md (Table) and the
   dev host's TableDemo. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Sorting and paging</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Sortable</Code> on a column and <Code>PageSize</Code> on the table. Headers and page numbers are links that set <Code>?sort=</Code> and <Code>?page=</Code>, so the table works with no script, survives a reload and can be shared.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-sort-page" />
<TextBlock><Code>InitialSort</Code> sets the order before anyone picks one (<Code>"-date"</Code> for newest first), and <Code>PageSizeOptions</Code> adds a "Rows per page" menu.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Column filters</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Filterable</Code> on a column for a filter in its header. Text matches anywhere, a date its day, enums and booleans get a menu, and a number takes a range: at least, at most, or both.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-filters" />
<TextBlock>Filters go in the URL as <Code>?f-customer=ada</Code> (here <Code>?orders-f-customer=ada</Code>: this table has a <Code>QueryPrefix</Code>, so it sorts and pages apart from the one above, as under <Anchor Href="docs/components/table#several-tables-on-a-page">Several tables on a page</Anchor>). A number's two ends go in as <Code>?f-total=100&amp;f-total-to=500</Code>, so the form needs no script. <Code>FilterMultiple</Code> lets an enum column match several values. For filters of your own, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/filter-form">FilterForm</Anchor> above the table.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Selection</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>SelectionMode="SelectionMode.Multiple"</Code> with an <Code>ItemKey</Code> for a checkbox per row (<Code>Single</Code> for a radio button). In a form, the checked rows post their keys, so this works with JavaScript off.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-select-rows" />
<TextBlock>Read them as a <Code>Selection</Code> with <Code>[SupplyParameterFromForm(Name = "selected")]</Code> on a static page, or <Code>@@bind-Selection</Code> on an interactive one; <Code>Apply</Code> gives the selected items from a query. Give columns <Code>FooterText</Code> for a totals row.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="3">Bulk actions</Heading>
<TextBlock>While rows are selected, a bar above them shows how many, with <Code>Clear selection</Code> and the buttons you put in <Code>SelectionActions</Code>. The selection stays as you page, sort and filter. Once every row on the page is selected, the bar offers every row the filters match (<Code>Select all 23</Code>): the <Code>Selection</Code> then holds the filter and the rows left out, so <Code>Apply</Code> finds them without the page loading them. A change of filter clears it. Shift-click a checkbox to select or clear the rows from the last one clicked. With JavaScript off, only the rows checked on the page post.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-bulk-actions" />

<Heading Level="2">State in the component</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>@@bind-Sort</Code>, <Code>@@bind-Page</Code> and <Code>@@bind-Filter</Code> on an interactive page to keep them in C# instead of the URL: for a table in a dialog, or anywhere the address shouldn't change.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-bound" />
<TextBlock>The table starts from the URL if it has values, and a new sort or filter goes back to page 1. Set the page back to 1 yourself when your own filters change.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>The filter is a <Code>Filter</Code>: <Code>Filter.All</Code> of one condition per filtered column (contains for text, between for a number, equals for a date, equals or in for a choice). Set one in C# to filter from code. A condition no column's panel can show, such as an or, still applies, and shows as a tag that removes it:</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@BoundFilter</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Data from a database</Heading>
<TextBlock>Pass an EF Core <Code>IQueryable</Code> as <Code>Items</Code>, and the database sorts, filters and pages. For more control, use <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> to load the rows yourself. It gets a <Code>DataRequest</Code> (the range as <Code>Skip</Code> and <Code>Take</Code>, the <Code>Sort</Code>, the <Code>Filter</Code>, and the columns, which say how each key reads an item) and returns a <Code>DataResult</Code>. <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> counts what the filter keeps and lists the page, through EF Core's async methods:</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Provider</Code>
<TextBlock>The filter becomes a LINQ expression on each column's own property, so the database filters, and a value typed into a filter never becomes code. <Code>request.Apply(query)</Code> does the same without counting; <Code>ApplyFiltering</Code> and <Code>ApplySorting</Code> do one part each. To filter a column your own way, such as a value stored differently from how it's shown, give it a <Code>Filter</Code> that returns the condition:</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@CustomCondition</Code>
<TextBlock>When the rows come from an API, pass the request on. <Code>request.Filter?.Find("status")</Code> reads one column's condition, and <Code>request.ToQueryString()</Code> writes the whole request (<Code>sort=-date&amp;skip=20&amp;take=10&amp;filter=total ge 100</Code>). The API reads it back with <Code>DataRequest&lt;Order&gt;.Parse</Code> and applies it through the fields it declares, so a request can't reach other properties. <Code>ToODataQuery()</Code> writes it for an OData endpoint, and the request also serializes with <Code>System.Text.Json</Code>. <Anchor Href="docs/common/data">Data and filters</Anchor> has the operators, the filter syntax and a full client and server.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock><Code>DataFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</Code> declares every public property, so read it from a type made for the API, or leave a property out with <Code>[ScaffoldColumn(false)]</Code>. The request asks for its own <Code>Take</Code>, so cap it on the server if it shouldn't ask for every row.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="csharp">@OverTheWire</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Paging without a total</Heading>
<TextBlock>When the data pages by cursor (an opaque "next" token, as many APIs and logs do) or has no total, set <Code>Paging</Code> and return the next cursor from your <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>. The table passes it back as <Code>request.Cursor</Code>; the total is optional.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Cursor</Code>
<TextBlock><Code>DataPaging.NextPrevious</Code> shows First, Previous and Next. Return <Code>Previous</Code> too if your source has it; without it, interactive tables go back through the cursors they've followed, and static ones offer First.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-cursor-pages" />
<TextBlock><Code>DataPaging.LoadOnScroll</Code> loads the next page as the last row comes into view and adds it under the rows: in place on interactive pages, and on static pages by following its Load more link, which enhanced navigation swaps in with the scroll kept. Give the table a <Code>MaxHeight</Code>, so it scrolls in its own box and what's under it stays within reach. Its Load more button, under the last row, is for the keyboard, out of sight until it has focus (<Code>LoadMoreText</Code> sets its text); with JavaScript off it shows, as a link that reloads the table with one more page of rows (<Code>?page=3</Code> shows the first three, asked of the provider as one request). With an <Code>ItemKey</Code>, a row already shown isn't added twice. A parent's re-render keeps the added pages; call <Code>ReloadAsync()</Code> to start over. Both modes also work with <Code>Items</Code>, where the table pages by row offset. (A visible Load more button is a list's pattern: see ListView.)</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-load-on-scroll" />

<Heading Level="2">When loading fails</Heading>
<TextBlock>If the <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> throws (or an <Code>IQueryable</Code> in <Code>Items</Code> does), the table logs the exception and shows "Couldn't load the rows." with Retry in place of the rows. On static pages and with JavaScript off, Retry is a link to the same address. <Code>ErrorContent</Code> replaces the message. When loading on scroll, the rows already shown stay, and the message and Retry go under them. A request the table canceled because a newer one replaced it isn't an error: pass <Code>request.CancellationToken</Code> on to stop the work.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-load-error" />

<Heading Level="2">Row links</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>RowHref</Code> to make each row a link, and <Code>RowLabel</Code> to name it. Links, buttons and checkboxes inside the row still work on their own. Use <Code>RowCurrent</Code> to mark the row the page is showing, such as the one whose details are beside the table: it's drawn as selected and screen readers hear it as current (<Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor> has one with its details in an aside).</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@RowLinks</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Several tables on a page</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>QueryPrefix</Code> to give each table its own keys in the URL, so they sort and page on their own.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Prefix</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Lines, size and scrolling</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Size</Code>, <Code>Striped</Code>, <Code>ColumnLines</Code>, <Code>RowLines</Code> and <Code>Bordered</Code> for the look; turn <Code>Bordered</Code> off for a table in a card. <Code>MaxHeight</Code> scrolls the rows under a sticky header, and <Code>EmptyText</Code> shows when there are none.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Look</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

